-**V-6 — The opcode stays live across the computed jump, so one handler serves a family.** Opcodes
-`0x0A`–`0x0F` are six **24-byte** blocks with an identical opening that call one comparator; the
-discriminating value is the opcode itself, still in `r12b` after `jmp rcx`. *The dispatch does not
-consume the opcode, and a reimplementation that treats each target as a self-contained handler must
-still thread it through.*
-
-**V-7 — One hundred handlers, ONE program-counter advance.** Every dispatch path converges on the
-epilogue at `0025E60A`, which reloads the saved opcode, looks its length up in the table at
-`01EFEA60` (**stride 24, signed `word`**), advances the PC and loops:
-
-```asm
-0025E613  movzx edx, byte ptr [rsp + 0xb8]              ; the opcode, saved at 0025E5A1
-0025E62A  lea   rax, [rip - 0x13e631]                   ; 00120000
-0025E631  movsx rax, word ptr [rax + rcx*8 + 0x1ddea60] ; length table, op*24
-0025E641  add   r14, rax                                ; THE advance
-0025E644  jmp   0025e570                                ; next instruction
-```
-
-**This is why V-4's six inert opcodes and every out-of-range opcode still make progress**: they jump
-straight to `0025E60A`, do no work, and are advanced by the same epilogue as everything else. *An
-implementation that puts the advance inside each handler has 100 places to get it wrong; this one
-has one.*
-
-**V-8 — The advance is UNCONDITIONAL, and a handler that needs a different program counter cancels
-it in advance.** Every one of the **21** `sub r14` sites in the body is immediately preceded by the
-**same length-table lookup** — `lea rcx, [rax + rax*2]` then
-`movsx rax, word ptr [r8 + rcx*8 + 0x1ddea60]`. Not one of them is a different quantity. So a handler
-does not *choose* whether to advance; it subtracts the length it knows the epilogue is about to add.
-Three shapes use it:
-
-| shape | sites | what precedes the cancel |
-|---|---:|---|
-| **repeat / wait** | 10 | `mov word ptr [rsi + 0x14], 1` on nine of them, and `or byte ptr [r15 + 0x14], 0x40` on opcode `0x03` — set a state flag, cancel, **re-execute next frame** |
-| **jump** | 5 | `mov r14, qword ptr [rax + rcx*8]` — load the target, cancel, so the epilogue's add lands exactly on it |
-| **return / call** | 3 | `mov r14, qword ptr [r15 + 0x58]` then `add r14, rdx` around the cancel |
-
